{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:56:18Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 15, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "1\t# Spec: Clear all completed tasks at once\n2\t\n3\tTaiga #9\n4\t\n5\t## Workspace check\n6\t\n7\t`git status` is clean, `git log` shows the branch tip (`9c6eea4`) is the\n8\talready-merged \"Keep tasks when the server restarts\" (Taiga #5) work. No\n9\tforeign in-progress work in the tree. `docs/spec.md` and `docs/design.md`\n10\twere leftover from that finished Taiga #5 cycle, already merged, so there's\n11\tnothing to lose. This spec overwrites `spec.md`; `design.md` is left as-is\n12\tfor the ux-designer stage to overwrite in turn.\n13\t\n14\t## Triage\n15\t\n16\t**Feature** \u2014 net-new capability. Touches three layers (store, route,\n17\tfrontend), but each layer is a thin, additive slice of an established\n18\tpattern already used by every other mutation (`complete_task`,\n19\t`delete_task`, etc.) \u2014 there's no new architectural concept to introduce.\n20\tKeeping this as one spec rather than splitting: the \"split across layers\"\n21\trule exists to stop one cycle from carrying disproportionate weight, and\n22\there the backend half is ~15 lines across two files and the frontend half is\n23\tone new ~20-line component plus two call sites. Splitting would produce two\n24\treviews of trivial, mutually-meaningless partial features.\n25\t\n26\t## Goal\n27\t\n28\tLet me remove every completed task in one action, instead of deleting them\n29\tone at a time.\n30\t\n31\t## Acceptance criteria\n32\t\n33\t1. **Given** zero completed tasks exist, **when** I look at the clear-completed\n34\t   control, **then** it is disabled and shows a count of 0; clicking it (if\n35\t   somehow triggered) makes no API call and removes nothing.\n36\t2. **Given** N completed tasks and M open tasks (N > 0), **when** I trigger\n37\t   clear-completed, **then** exactly N tasks are removed, all M open tasks\n38\t   are unchanged (same id, title, priority, done=false), and the control's\n39\t   count reflects N before the action runs.\n40\t3. **Given** clear-completed has just run, **when** I `GET /api/tasks`,\n41\t   **then** no task in the response has `done: true`.\n42\t4. **Given** the current list filter is \"open\" or \"done\" (not \"all\"),\n43\t   **when** I trigger clear-completed, **then** it still removes every\n44\t   completed task store-wide \u2014 the action is not scoped to what's currently\n45\t   visible under the filter.\n46\t5. **Given** clear-completed has just run, **when** the server restarts\n47\t   (store reloads from `TASKS_DATA_FILE`), **then** the removed tasks do not\n48\t   reappear \u2014 the on-disk snapshot reflects the removal, same guarantee\n49\t   every other mutation already gives (Taiga #5).\n50\t6. **Given** the task list is entirely empty (no tasks at all, not just no\n51\t   completed ones), **when** `DELETE /api/tasks/completed` is called\n52\t   directly, **then** it returns `200` with a count of `0`, not an error.\n53\t\n54\t## Non-goals\n55\t\n56\t- No undo. This mirrors the existing per-task Delete button, which is also\n57\t  irreversible.\n58\t- No confirmation modal / `window.confirm` dialog. Nothing else in this app\n59\t  interrupts a destructive action with a popup (per-task Delete doesn't\n60\t  either); \"tells me how many it will remove\" is satisfied by the count\n61\t  being visible on the control before it's clicked, not by a popup.\n62\t- No selective clearing (by priority, by age, by title match) \u2014 \"every\n63\t  completed task,\" full stop.\n64\t- No keyboard shortcut.\n65\t- No change to how individual tasks are completed or deleted.\n66\t\n67\t## Affected areas\n68\t\n69\t- `backend/app/store.py` \u2014 add `TaskStore.clear_completed() -> int`,\n70\t  following the exact shape of `delete()`/`set_done()`: mutate `self._tasks`,\n71\t  call `self._save()` once if anything changed, return the number removed.\n72\t- `backend/app/models.py` \u2014 add a response model (no bare dicts, per\n73\t  convention), e.g. `ClearResult(BaseModel)` with a single `count: int`\n74\t  field.\n75\t- `backend/app/main.py` \u2014 add `DELETE /api/tasks/completed`, response_model\n76\t  `ClearResult`, calling `store.clear_completed()`.\n77\t  **Ordering hazard, confirmed while reading this file**: the existing\n78\t  `@app.delete(\"/api/tasks/{task_id}\")` at line 56 has no `:int` converter\n79\t  in the path string, so Starlette matches `/api/tasks/completed` against it\n80\t  as a plain string first (converter mismatch happens at\n81\t  FastAPI's pydantic-validation stage, not at route-matching), producing a\n82\t  `422` for `task_id=\"completed\"` instead of ever reaching a route\n83\t  registered later. The new route **must** be declared above line 56, not\n84\t  below it.\n85\t- `backend/tests/test_tasks.py` \u2014 new tests alongside the existing\n86\t  `test_<behavior>` style (see e.g. lines 34\u201341 for the delete-single\n87\t  pattern).\n88\t- `frontend/src/api.js` \u2014 add `clearCompleted = () => request('/api/tasks/completed', { method: 'DELETE' })`, same one-liner shape as the existing exports.\n89\t- `frontend/src/components/` \u2014 one new component file for the control\n90\t  (one component per file, per project convention). Exact placement/copy is\n91\t  the ux-designer's call; functionally it needs the completed count and an\n92\t  onClear handler.\n93\t- `frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 compute `completedCount = tasks.filter(t =>\n94\t  t.done).length` (note: from the full `tasks` list, not `visibleTasks` \u2014\n95\t  ties to AC4, the control's count must not be filter-scoped), render the\n96\t  new control, wire its handler through the existing `run()` helper\n97\t  (line 37) so errors surface through the existing `error` banner\n98\t  (line 52) the same way every other action already does.\n99\t- `frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 styling for the new control, matching existing\n100\t  button conventions (lines 16\u201323).\n101\t\n102\t## Edge cases\n103\t\n104\t- Zero completed tasks: control is inert (AC1). No API call fires.\n105\t- All tasks are completed: list becomes empty afterward, same empty-state\n106\t  copy path already used elsewhere (`TaskList`'s `EMPTY_COPY`).\n107\t- Mixed completed/open: only completed removed, open tasks' ids/order\n108\t  untouched (AC2).\n109\t- Action triggered while a filter other than \"all\" is active: still\n110\t  store-wide (AC4).\n111\t- Backend restart after clearing: removal persists (AC5).\n112\t- Direct API call against an already-empty store: `200`/count `0`, not a\n113\t  404 or error (AC6) \u2014 there's no per-item id here to 404 against, unlike\n114\t  the single-delete route.\n115\t- Network/API failure mid-clear: surfaces through the existing `error` state\n116\t  in `App.jsx`, same as every other mutation \u2014 no new error-handling path\n117\t  needed.\n118\t\n119\t## Open questions\n120\t\n121\t- **How the count is communicated** (\"tells me how many it will remove\"):\n122\t  proceeding under the assumption that an inline, always-visible count on\n123\t  the control (e.g. a label showing the number) satisfies this, rather than\n124\t  a confirmation dialog \u2014 justified above under Non-goals, since no other\n125\t  destructive action in this app uses a dialog. If the actual product intent\n126\t  was a pre-delete confirmation step, that's a different, larger UI pattern\n127\t  this app doesn't have anywhere yet and would need sign-off before the\n128\t  ux-designer builds it \u2014 override here if that reading is wrong.\n129\t- **Response shape for the bulk-clear endpoint**: proceeding under a new\n130\t  `ClearResult{count: int}` model (200 OK) rather than reusing `Task`'s\n131\t  204-no-content convention from single delete, because this route has no\n132\t  single task id to report \u2014 flagging in case a different contract (e.g.\n133\t  returning the list of removed task ids) is wanted for some downstream\n134\t  consumer; nothing in this codebase currently needs that.\n135\t", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01SFsBb4Ao62fSLVW418uFPU", "session_id": "227f188c-d9da-4a93-804a-9521f303ed03"}}
