{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:57:53Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 69, "kind": "tool_use", "text": "Write", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "tool_use", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01LoGMFpxBrT9X8zsk7orh2v", "input": {"file_path": "/home/dev/projects/test-project/docs/design.md", "content": "# Design: Clear all completed tasks at once\n\nTaiga #9 \u00b7 for `docs/spec.md` (same ticket)\n\n## Conventions found (establishing the stack)\n\n- Plain function components, one per file, under `frontend/src/components/`.\n- Plain CSS in `frontend/src/App.css`, no framework, no CSS-in-JS, no design\n  token layer \u2014 colors and spacing are hand-picked literals reused by eye\n  (e.g. `#ccd2d6` for every border, `0.5rem 0.8rem` for every button's\n  padding).\n- Every button is a bare `<button>` using the global `button { \u2026 }` /\n  `button:disabled { opacity: 0.5; cursor: not-allowed; }` rules\n  (`frontend/src/App.css:16-23`). No button anywhere has its own color \u2014\n  `Edit`, `Done`, `Delete`, filter options, and `Add` all look the same\n  modulo the `.filter-option.active` state. **There is no \"destructive =\n  red\" convention in this app** \u2014 the existing per-task `Delete` button\n  (`TaskList.jsx:110-112`) is styled identically to `Edit`/`Done`.\n- No modal, `<dialog>`, or overlay pattern exists anywhere in this codebase.\n  Confirms the spec's non-goal: this control must not introduce one.\n- Handler-prop naming is consistently `on<Verb>` (`onComplete`, `onDelete`,\n  `onUpdate`, `onChange`) \u2014 new prop is `onClear`.\n- `TaskFilter` (`frontend/src/components/TaskFilter.jsx`) is the closest\n  sibling in shape: a small standalone control rendered directly in\n  `App.jsx` between the error banner and the list, not owned by any other\n  component.\n- No focus-ring override exists in `App.css` \u2014 browser default focus\n  styling is used app-wide. Not introducing one here either.\n- No in-flight/spinner state exists on any button in this app (`Add`,\n  `Complete`, `Delete`, `Save` all fire-and-await via `run()` with no busy\n  indicator). Not introducing one here either, for consistency.\n\n## New component\n\n`frontend/src/components/ClearCompleted.jsx` \u2014 one file, per convention.\n\n```jsx\nexport default function ClearCompleted({ count, onClear }) {\n  return (\n    <button\n      type=\"button\"\n      className=\"clear-completed\"\n      disabled={count === 0}\n      onClick={onClear}\n    >\n      Clear completed ({count})\n    </button>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nProps: `count` (number), `onClear` (handler, no args \u2014 matches the\nzero-arg shape `onClear={() => run(() => api.clearCompleted())}` will take\nin `App.jsx`, same pattern as `AddTask`'s `onAdd`).\n\nNo `aria-label` needed: unlike `Edit`/`Delete`/`Complete` in `TaskList`,\nwhich need one because their visible text (\"Delete\") doesn't say *which*\ntask, this button's own text already states the full action and count \u2014\nthat text **is** its accessible name.\n\n## Placement and layout\n\nRender as a sibling of `TaskFilter`, not nested inside it \u2014 this is a\ndeliberate visual signal for **AC4** (the action is store-wide, not\nfilter-scoped): if it lived inside or under the filter control it would\nvisually imply \"clear completed *within this filter view*,\" which is\nfalse. Introduce one new wrapper row so both sit on one line, right-aligned\nagainst the filter:\n\n`frontend/src/App.jsx` \u2014 replace the standalone `<TaskFilter .../>` line\nwith:\n\n```jsx\n<div className=\"list-toolbar\">\n  <TaskFilter value={filter} onChange={setFilter} />\n  <ClearCompleted count={completedCount} onClear={() => run(() => api.clearCompleted())} />\n</div>\n```\n\nwhere `completedCount = tasks.filter((t) => t.done).length`, computed from\nthe full `tasks` array (already specified in `docs/spec.md`'s Affected\nareas) \u2014 same value in both the \"before action\" display (AC2) and the\n\"store-wide, not view-scoped\" guarantee (AC4), because it never reads\n`visibleTasks`.\n\n`frontend/src/App.css` \u2014 new rule:\n\n```css\n.list-toolbar {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\n  gap: 0.6rem;\n  margin-bottom: 1.2rem;\n}\n.list-toolbar .task-filter { margin-bottom: 0; }\n```\n\n`.task-filter` already carries `margin-bottom: 1.2rem` (`App.css:38`) for\nits old standalone placement; the override above prevents doubled spacing\nnow that the parent `.list-toolbar` owns that margin. `ClearCompleted`'s\nbutton needs no new rule of its own \u2014 it inherits the global `button` /\n`button:disabled` styling, which is the point (no new visual language for\n\"destructive,\" per the convention check above).\n\n`flex-wrap: wrap` is the adaptive behavior for the narrow (`max-width:\n34rem`, effectively mobile-width) container: if both controls don't fit\none line, `ClearCompleted` drops to its own line below `TaskFilter` rather\nthan truncating or overlapping. No breakpoint needed \u2014 the container is\nalready narrow at every viewport.\n\n## States\n\n**Empty (0 completed) \u2014 AC1**\n```\n[ All | Open | Done ]         [ Clear completed (0) ]   \u2190 disabled, dimmed\n```\n`disabled` is set directly from `count === 0`; no click handler runs, so\n\"clicking it (if somehow triggered) makes no API call\" is structurally\ntrue \u2014 a disabled `<button>` fires no `onClick` in the DOM, not just\nvisually inert.\n\n**Loading (initial fetch, before first `refresh()` resolves)**\n\n`TaskFilter` already renders unconditionally above the\n`loading ? <p className=\"loading\">\u2026</p> : <TaskList .../>` branch in\n`App.jsx` (lines 53-65) \u2014 it doesn't wait for data. `ClearCompleted` joins\nit in the same unconditional `.list-toolbar` row, for the same reason:\n`tasks` starts as `[]`, so `completedCount` is `0` and the button renders\ndisabled. This is visually identical to the empty state above and that's\ncorrect \u2014 before any data has loaded, \"0 completed\" is truthful, not a\nplaceholder guess. No separate loading treatment for this control.\n\n**Populated \u2014 AC2**\n```\n[ All | Open | Done ]         [ Clear completed (3) ]   \u2190 enabled\n```\nOne completed: `Clear completed (1)`. Many completed (e.g. 47): the label\nis just a number, no truncation/\"99+\" logic needed \u2014 nothing in the spec\nor this app's scale implies large counts.\n\n**All tasks completed, then cleared**\n\nList becomes empty after the action. No new empty-state copy: the\nexisting `EMPTY_COPY` in `TaskList.jsx:3-7` already handles it per the\nactive filter (`\"Nothing to do yet.\"` / `\"Nothing open right now.\"` /\n`\"Nothing done yet.\"`) \u2014 this path was already reachable via one-at-a-time\nDelete, so it's already correct.\n\n**Error (API call fails mid-clear)**\n\nNo new UI. `onClear` is wired through the existing `run()` helper\n(`App.jsx:37-46`), which on rejection calls `setError(e.message)` and\nrenders the existing `<p className=\"error\" role=\"alert\">` banner\n(`App.jsx:52`) \u2014 identical path to a failed `Delete`. The button itself\nreturns to its normal enabled state (no local pending flag is introduced,\nso there's nothing to reset). `count` is unchanged because `refresh()`\nnever ran, so the pre-click count is still shown, which is the truthful\ncount.\n\n**Interactive**\n\n- *Default / hover*: base `button` styling, no new rule \u2014 matches\n  `Edit`/`Done`/`Delete`.\n- *Focus*: browser default outline, unmodified (no `:focus` rule exists\n  anywhere in `App.css` to be consistent with).\n- *Disabled*: `button:disabled { opacity: 0.5; cursor: not-allowed; }`\n  (`App.css:23`), the exact rule already applied to `Add` when its input is\n  empty. This reuses an existing token/rule verbatim rather than\n  introducing a new color pairing, so no fresh contrast computation\n  applies \u2014 the enabled-state pairing it inherits from is checked below.\n\n## Accessibility\n\n- **Target size**: same `padding: 0.5rem 0.8rem` as every other button in\n  the app (~36px effective height at the default 16px body font) \u2014 no new\n  sizing decision, reusing the established value.\n- **Focus order**: `ClearCompleted` sits immediately after `TaskFilter`'s\n  three buttons in DOM order (sibling in `.list-toolbar`, declared second),\n  so tab order is All \u2192 Open \u2192 Done \u2192 Clear completed \u2192 (task rows) \u2014\n  matches visual left-to-right reading order.\n- **Contrast** (enabled state, the pairing every other button in this app\n  already uses \u2014 verifying it here since this is the first time it's being\n  stated numerically): text `#16191c` on background `#fff`, both existing\n  literals from `App.css`. Relative luminance: `#16191c` \u2248 0.0097, `#fff` =\n  1.0. Contrast ratio = (1.0 + 0.05) / (0.0097 + 0.05) \u2248 **17.5:1** \u2014 passes\n  the 4.5:1 text threshold with large margin. Border `#ccd2d6` on `#fff`\n  background (non-text, 3:1 threshold): luminance of `#ccd2d6` \u2248 0.716,\n  ratio = (1.0 + 0.05) / (0.716 + 0.05) \u2248 **1.37:1** \u2014 this fails 3:1, but\n  it is the exact same border already used on every other button, input,\n  and the filter group in this app; not a new pairing introduced by this\n  feature, so there's nothing to adjust here without relitigating the\n  whole app's existing button chrome, which is out of scope for this\n  ticket.\n- **Screen reader**: accessible name is the full button text, \"Clear\n  completed (N)\" \u2014 states the action and the count together, so no\n  separate `aria-live` announcement is needed for \"how many it will\n  remove\" (AC2's requirement is satisfied by the visible/accessible label\n  itself, not a dynamic announcement).\n\n## Acceptance criteria traceability\n\n| AC | Design element |\n|----|-----------------|\n| 1 (0 completed \u2192 disabled, count 0, inert) | `disabled={count === 0}` in `ClearCompleted.jsx`; Empty state above |\n| 2 (count shown before action runs) | Label reads `count` from render-time `completedCount`, which only changes after `refresh()` completes post-`onClear` |\n| 3 (no `done:true` after) | Backend-only, no visual dimension |\n| 4 (store-wide, not filter-scoped) | `ClearCompleted` is a sibling of `TaskFilter`, not nested in/gated by it; `completedCount` sourced from full `tasks`, not `visibleTasks` \u2014 see Placement section |\n| 5 (persists across restart) | Backend-only, no visual dimension |\n| 6 (empty store \u2192 200/count 0) | Backend-only; front-end behavior is identical to the Empty state (button already disabled, no call ever fires) |\n\n## What this does not touch\n\n- No confirmation dialog \u2014 non-goal, and no precedent exists in this app to\n  build one from.\n- No new color, icon, or button variant for \"destructive\" actions \u2014\n  `Delete`, and now `Clear completed`, both use the same plain button as\n  every non-destructive action.\n- `TaskList`'s empty-state copy is unchanged; the existing per-filter\n  strings already cover the post-clear empty case.\n"}, "session_id": "227f188c-d9da-4a93-804a-9521f303ed03"}}
